    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d458">

                <pb facs="0573" n="561" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg561"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/6707: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-16T19:00:00+02:00">November 16,
                            1940—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 17—5:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>311. A British squadron of 5 warships debarked troops apparently from Palestine
                    and Egypt at Piraeus this afternoon. According to the British Consul there, the
                    troops number “several thousand” and more are expected. Hundreds have already
                    been brought to Athens for temporary billets, receiving an ecstatic reception
                    from the people.</p>
                <p>Department’s telegrams 255 and 256<anchor n="48" xml:id="d458-f1"/> received
                    yesterday. Nos. 257 to 261<anchor n="49" xml:id="d458-f2"/> inclusive received
                    today.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
